  "intro": "The U.S. exported **$77.66B** of plastics and plastic articles in 2025, down 3.0% from 2024 and 7% below the 2022 peak of $83.28B. Resin dominates: polyethylene and other ethylene polymers (3901) alone were **21%** ($16.51B), and finished articles (3926, 3923, 3920) add another quarter. Mexico and Canada took **46%** between them, and Mexico ($21.09B) is the single largest buyer.\n\nTariffs are a minor factor for most buyers: Mexico, Canada, Singapore and South Korea charge nothing on qualifying goods, and the EU cut its tariff from 6.5% to zero on July 1, 2026. The exceptions are **China**, which adds a 10% extra duty to its MFN rate (and a 74.9% anti-dumping duty on U.S. POM copolymer) and bought 19.7% less, and **Canada**, which since September 2026 surtaxes six consumer-plastic tariff items at 50%. What usually decides access is chemical-inventory law and food-contact rules, not the tariff.",
